-- title:
--   A global index retraction for representatives of pairwise disjoint blocks
-- statement:
--   Let $\alpha$ be a type and $\iota$ an inhabited index type. Let $\mathrm{Blk}$ be a family of subsets of $\alpha$ and let
--
--   $$\rho : \mathcal{P}(\alpha) \times \iota \to \alpha, \qquad (B, i) \mapsto \rho_B(i)$$
--
--   be a choice of representatives satisfying, for every $B \in \mathrm{Blk}$:
--
--   - **membership:** $\rho_B(i) \in B$ for every index $i$;
--   - **injectivity:** $i \mapsto \rho_B(i)$ is injective;
--
--   and let the blocks of $\mathrm{Blk}$ be pairwise disjoint, in the form: if $B, B' \in \mathrm{Blk}$ share a point then $B = B'$. Then the index is globally recoverable from the representative: there is a map
--
--   $$\mathrm{idx} : \alpha \to \iota \qquad\text{with}\qquad \mathrm{idx}\bigl(\rho_B(i)\bigr) = i \quad\text{for all } B \in \mathrm{Blk},\ i \in \iota .$$
--
--   The content is that the per-block inverses of $\rho_B$ glue into a single function defined on all of $\alpha$: disjointness of the blocks means a point determines the block it can come from, and injectivity then determines the index. Having $\mathrm{idx}$ globally defined — rather than only on the union of the representative sets — is what allows the index to be read off an arbitrary point encountered in later constructions.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** $\rho$ takes an arbitrary set as its first argument, with no hypotheses outside $\mathrm{Blk}$; $\mathrm{idx}$ is total on $\alpha$ and takes an unspecified value (supplied by the `Inhabited ι` instance) at points that are not representatives.
